{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]],"date-time":"2026-09-04T10:20:36Z","timestamp":1788517236471,"version":"build-2803163510"},"reference-count":76,"publisher":"Wiley","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]],"date-time":"2026-09-04T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1788480000000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"},{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]],"date-time":"2026-09-04T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1788480000000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/doi.wiley.com\/10.1002\/tdm_license_1.1"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["Legal Criminol Psychol"],"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Purpose<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>Evidence suggests that prisoners have more extensive victimization histories than non\u2010incarcerated people, which is linked to subsequent criminal behaviour. Additionally, violence and victimization are common in prisons and often underreported. Prior research has primarily focussed on the negative effects of victimization prior to incarceration. Thus, this review will explore the impact of victimization in prison on post\u2010release outcomes.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Methods<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>A systematic literature search was conducted on PsycINFO, Web of Science, Scopus and Social Science Premium databases. Any peer\u2010reviewed studies written in English were eligible for inclusion if they analysed the relationship between victimization exposure in prison on post\u2010release outcomes or recidivism. Methodological quality was assessed using CASP Checklists. Of the 322 unique studies identified, 18 studies were selected based on the inclusion criteria.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Results<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>High rates of direct and witnessed victimization were reported. The majority of the quantitative studies found significant relationships between prison victimization and recidivism or other post\u2010release outcomes. Stronger evidence suggests recidivism, substance use and mental health outcomes are correlated with prison victimization, while evidence for interpersonal relationships and employment outcomes is weaker. Qualitative studies supported these findings and drew associations between prison violence and poorer outcomes upon release.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Conclusions<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>The results of this study indicate that trauma experienced while incarcerated is associated with greater risk of reoffending or other poor outcomes. However, the studies were limited to primarily adult samples in the United States, with mostly male participants; future research should seek to examine these effects in other populations.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>","DOI":"10.1111\/lcrp.70059","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]],"date-time":"2026-09-04T10:10:02Z","timestamp":1788516602000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/crossmark_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Prison victimization and post\u2010release outcomes: A systematic review"],"prefix":"10.1111","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7891-3147","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Caitlyn","family":"Rawers","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Psychology Ulster University  Coleraine UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0477-2297","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Susan","family":"Lagdon","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Psychology Ulster University  Coleraine UK"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"311","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]]},"reference":[{"key":"e_1_2_10_2_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.chiabu.2023.106055"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_3_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1177\/0886260519876021"},{"issue":"2","key":"e_1_2_10_4_1","article-title":"The prevalence of adverse childhood experiences (ACE) in the lives of juvenile offenders","volume":"3","author":"Baglivio M. T.","year":"2014","journal-title":"Journal of Juvenile Justice"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_5_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/s10488\u2010009\u20100252\u20109"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_6_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.6000\/1929\u20104409.2017.06.05"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_7_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1111\/j.1468\u20102311.2004.00316.x"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_8_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1080\/10926771.2015.1133749"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_9_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1086\/718515"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_10_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.paid.2018.04.023"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_11_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1177\/0093854809336453"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_12_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1080\/15564886.2019.1627683"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_13_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1080\/15564886.2020.1787283"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_14_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1080\/15564880601087241"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_15_1","unstructured":"Cantor D. &Lynch J. P.(2000).Self\u2010Report Surveys as Measures of Crime and Criminal Victimization. (Criminal Justice 2000 4 pp. 85\u2013139).https:\/\/www.ojp.gov\/criminal_justice2000\/vol_4\/04front.pdf"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_16_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1177\/1466138112457299"},{"key":"e_1_2_10_17_1","doi-asserted-by":"publis